 ago; ambitious conjecture was that the number of transversals of a latin square is equivalent to ... Rodney [4, p 105] conjectured that every latin square has a duplex.\nRecalling that the original sum must be 0, we now have\n0 = (ku −ks) −(ku −kr) −(ku −kc)\n= −ku + k(s −r −c).\nThus k(s −r −c) = ku. By Lemma 1, if k is even or G does not have a unique involution,\nthen ku = 0. Otherwise, k is odd and u is an involution. Therefore ku = u.\n3\nThe Existence and Non-existence of k-weights\nRyser conjectured that every latin square of odd order has a transversal. His original, more\nambitious conjecture was that the number of transversals of a latin square is equivalent to\nthe square’s order modulo 2. Balasubramanian proved this form of the conjecture for even\norders [2] but it fails for many latin squares of odd order beginning with 7. As an aside,\nAkbari and Alipour have extended Balasubramanian’s method to show that in any latin\nsquare the number of maximal near transversals is equivalent to 0 modulo 4 [1].\nRodney [4, p 105] conjectured that every latin square has a duplex. Both Ryser’s and\nRodney’s conjectures have been shown by computer search to hold at least up to order 9 by\n[14] and [8], respectively, and both are subsumed by a stronger conjecture again attributed\nto Rodney that every latin square can be partitioned into a collection of transversals and\nduplexes. For a more detailed discussion of the origins of these conjectures one should consult\n[8].\nAs an aside, a weaker but potentially more tractable question is whether every latin\nsquare can be partitioned into proper k-plexes, i.e. does every latin square of order n have a\nk-plex for some k < n? The answer to even this seemingly weak question remains unknown.\nOur ﬁrst theorem shows that the natural analogues of Ryser’s and Rodney’s conjectures\nhold for k-weights.\nTheorem 3. Every latin square has a 2-weight and those of odd order have 1-weights.\nProof. Fix any cell (r, c, s) ∈L and deﬁne θ : L →Z as follows:\n\n\n\nθ(x, y, z) =\n3 −n\nif (x, y, z) = (r, c, s)\n1\nif (x, y, z) and (r, c, s) agree in precisely one position\n0\notherwise.\n\n\nWe claim that θ is a 2-weight of L. First consider the sum of θ over row x ̸= r. All cells in\nrow x have been assigned 0 except for the cell in column c and the cell containing symbol s.\nThese exceptions must be distinct cells since L is a latin square. Since these two cells both\ncarry a weight of 1, the sum over row x is 2. Now consider the sum over row r. Every cell\nin row r carries weight 1 except for cell (r, c, s), which carries weight 3 −n. Thus the sum\nover row r is (n −1) + (3 −n) = 2.\nSince our construction treats rows, columns, and symbols symmetrically, it follows that\nall column and symbol sums are also 2.\n4--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTalk 3\n\nProgress on Hippchen’s Conjecture\n\nPropose (https://www.ibs.re.kr/ecopro/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/2023-DC-workshop-program_final2.pdf)\nciteturn10search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Progress on Hippchen’s Conjecture ... Hippchen [3] conjectured that two longest paths in a k-connected graph share at least k vertices.\nTalk 3\n\nProgress on Hippchen’s Conjecture\n\nProposed by Ilkyoo Choi, Hankuk University of Foreign Studies\n\nHippchen [3] conjectured that two longest paths in a k-connected graph share at least k vertices. For each k, there are infinitely many graphs for which the conjecture, if true, is tight. (The complete bipartite graph K_{k,2k+2} is an example.) The conjecture is known to be true when k ≤ 5 or k ≥ (n+2)/5 [2].\n\nOn the other hand, two longest cycles in a k-connected graph share at least c k^{3/5} vertices [1], where c ≈ 0.2615. This can be used to prove that every two longest paths in a k-connected graph have at least common c k^{3/5} vertices.\n\nAny improvements would be interesting.\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Guantao Chen, Ralph J. Faudree, and Ronald J