ere is no vw-path internally disjoint from both P and Q.\nIntersection of longest paths in 4-connected graphs\n\nJuan Gutiérrez†\n\n†Department of Computer Science, Universidad de Ingeniería y Tecnología, Perú\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBlue section header: “Hippchen’s Conjecture”. To the right is a graph illustration with labeled paths/vertices P, Q, R, x, y, a, b, c. Caption: “Fig. 1: abc is a subsequence in Q.”\n</visual_element>\n\nKnown result: Every pair of longest paths in a connected graph intersect in at least one vertex.\n\nConjecture 1 ([2, Conjecture 2.2.4]). Every pair of longest paths in a k-connected graph intersect in at least k vertices.\n\n• Proved by himself for k = 3  \n• Our result: extended for k = 4  \n• A similar conjecture, for cycles instead of paths, was proposed by Grötschel and attributed to Scott Smith [1, Conjecture 5.2].\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nSecond graph illustration with labeled paths/vertices P, Q, R, x, y, a, b, c. Caption: “Fig. 2: acb is a subsequence in Q.”\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nBlue section header: “Auxiliar lemma”.\n</visual_element>\n\nLemma 2. Let P and Q be two longest paths in a graph G. Let u ∈ V(P) ∩ V(Q). Let v be a vertex in V(P) \\ V(Q) such that P[u,v] is internally disjoint from Q. Let w be a vertex in V(Q) \\ V(P) such that Q[u,w] is internally disjoint from P. Then, there is no vw-path internally disjoint from both P and Q.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e4\">\nBlue section header: “Tight families”. Text below: “Hippchen’s Conjecture is tight for any k: K_{k,2k+2}. We generalized this result.” To the right/below is a graph construction figure with many labeled vertices and edges, captioned: “Fig. 3: The graph used in the construction of Theorem 4 when ℓ = 2.”\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e5\">\nBlue section header: “Main theorem”.\n</visual_element>\n\nTheorem 3. Every pair of longest paths in a 4-connected graph intersect in at least four vertices.\n\nProof sketch.\n\n• Let P and Q two longest paths.  \n• Let {a,b,c} be the intersection of P and Q; suppose abc is a subsequence in P  \n• We divide the proof in two cases, where {a,b,c}\n\nCase 1. abc is a subsequence in Q.  \nCase 2. acb is a subsequence in Q.\n\n• In each case we define\n\nG′ = G − {a,b,c}  \nE(H) = {XY :  \nthere is a X-Y path in G′, with no internal vertex in V(P ∪ Q)}.\n\n• By the Auxiliar Lemma, some edges cannot exist in H, which lead us to a contradiction.\n\nTheorem 4. For every k-connected graph, there exists an infinite family of graphs with a pair of longest paths intersecting each other in exactly k vertices.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e6\">\nBlue section header: “References”.\n</visual_element>\n\n[1] M. Grötschel. “On intersections of longest cycles”. In: Graph Theory and Combinatorics (1984), pp. 171–189.  \n[2] T. Hippchen. “Intersections of Longest Paths and Cycles”. MA thesis. Georgia State University, 2008.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn two conjectures about the intersection of longest paths and cycles (https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03849)\nciteturn10academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Title: On two conjectures about the intersection of longest paths and cycles ... An analog conjecture for paths instead of cycles was stated by Hippchen.\nTitle: On two conjectures about the intersection of longest paths and cycles\nAuthors: Juan Gutiérrez, Christian Valqui\nDate: Thu Oct  5 19:18:19 2023\n\nA conjecture attributed to Smith states that every pair of longest cycles in a $k$-connected graph intersect each other in at least $k$ vertices. In this paper, we show that every pair of longest cycles in a~$k$-connected graph on $n$ vertices intersect each other in at least~$\\min\\{n,8k-n-16\\}$ vertices, which confirms Smith's conjecture when $k\\geq (n+16)/7$. An analog conjecture for paths instead of cycles was stated by Hippchen. By a simple reduction, we relate both conjectures, showing that Hippchen's conjecture is valid when either $k \\leq 6$ or $k \\geq (n+9)/7$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Harmonious sequences in groups with a unique involution (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395844934_Harmonious_sequences_in_groups_with_a_unique_involution)\nciteturn10search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The Ryser–Brualdi–Stein conjecture holds for group-based Latin squares [BGHJ91,GH20]. ... Rodney conjectured that every Latin square contains a duplex [CD06].\n\n0 g 1,g−1\n\n1 g 2, . . . , g−1\n\nm g 0 also form a permutation of non-\n\nidentity elements of G. Every abelian group is either sequenceable or R-sequenceable [AKP17].\n\nA group is called D-sequenceable, if there exists a sequence g in G such that every element\n\nof G appears exactly twice in each of g and ¯g. All abelian groups are D-sequenceable, and it\n\nis conjectured that all ﬁnite groups are D-sequenceable [Jav24]. We introduce the following\n\nharmonious counterpart of D-sequenceability.\n\nDeﬁnition 1.3. Let G be a group and A be a ﬁnite subset of G. We say g is a doubly harmonious\n\nsequence in A if every element of A appears exactly twice in each of g and ˆg, in which case we\n\nsay A is doubly harmonious.\n\nEvery doubly harmonious sequence in G gives rise to a cyclic duplex in L(G). A duplex\n\nin an n×n Latin square is a set of 2 n cells that contains two cells from each row, column,\n\n4 Lydia de Wolf, Mohammad Javaheri\n\nand symbol. Rodney conjectured that every Latin square contains a duplex [CD06]. Rodney’s\n\nconjecture has been veriﬁed for L(G)if G is solvable [VLW03].\n\nIn Section 3, we show that if G is an odd group (possibly trivial) and H is abelian, then G×H\n\nis doubly harmonious (Theorem 3.5), and so L(G×H)admits a cyclic duplex. In addition, we\n\nshow that if G is an abelian group of order at least 4, then G♯=G\\{0 G}is doubly harmonious\n\n--------------