irect the readers to the surveys [7], [10] for more details and related results.\n\nInstead of ensuring a common vertex in all (or many) longest paths, one could seek sufficient conditions that imply many common vertices in two longest paths. Hippchen [6] made the following intriguing conjecture, suggesting that the number of vertices shared by two longest paths in a graph G is at least the connectivity of G. Note that the complete bipartite graph where the two parts have k and vertices demonstrates that the conjecture, if it is true, is tight. There is also an infinite family of graphs for each k that demonstrates the tightness of the conjecture [5].\n\nConjecture 1.1 [6]\n\nIf G is a k-connected graph, then two longest paths in G share at least k vertices.\n\nHippchen [6] proved the above conjecture for , and recently, Gutiérrez [5] proved the conjecture for .\n\nTheorem 1.2 [5], [6]\n\nFor , Conjecture 1.1 is true.\n\nOur first result verifies Hippchen's Conjecture for the next open case, which was a conjecture in [5].\n\nTheorem 1.3\n\nFor , Conjecture 1.1 is true.\n\nGutiérrez also proved that the same conclusion holds whenever the connectivity is linear in terms of the number of vertices. Namely, for a k-connected graph G on at most vertices, two longest paths in G share at least k vertices. He actually proved the following stronger statement: If G is a k-connected graph on n vertices, then two longest paths in G share at least vertices. We improve upon the aforementioned statement in the following form:\n\nTheorem 1.4\n\nIf G is a k-connected graph on n vertices, then two longest paths in G share at least vertices.\n\nSince implies , we obtain the following corollary as a direct consequence of Theorem 1.4, thereby extending the class of graphs for which Hippchen's Conjecture is true. \nLemma 3.1\n\nLet G be a k-connected graph with . If P and Q are two longest paths in G, then P and Q share at least vertices, where .\n\nProof\n\nLet P and Q be longest paths i